LINUX LOGGING:
		|--> 	SYSLOG: Standard System Logging Protocol defined by RFC 5424, used across a variety of systems, and even on some enterprise Windows hosts
					JOURNALD: unique to systemd distributions of Linux.



SYSLOG DAEMON: logs stored here are human-readable within /VAR/LOG, configured using files in /etc/rsyslog/

configuration setup: (how the log is read):

							SELECTORS:												ACTIONS:
					FACILITY.SERVERITY      				/PATH/TO/LOG/LOCATION



FACILITY:event, that generated the log								#BOTH FACILITY AND SERVERITY USE NUMBERS 0-7 

SERVERITY:how urgent an event is from 0 TO 7 			

PATH/TO/LOG/LOCATION:where the log is stored and/or any action taken on the event before storage 

SYSLOG FACILITIES:																						SYSLOG SERVERITIES:
0				KERNEL MESSAGES																				0				EMERGENCY
1				USER-LEVEL MESSAGES																		1				ALERT
2				MAIL SYSTEM																						2				CRITICAL
3				SYSTEM DAEMONS																				3				ERROR
4				SEC/AUTH MESSAGES																			4				WARNING
5				MESSAGES MADE BY SYSLOG																5				NOTICE
6				LINE PRINTER SUBSYSTEM															  6				INFO
7				NETWORK NEWS SUBSYSTEM																7				DEBUG



